Mild localized contact dermatitis presenting as an erythematous patch from a new cosmetic product
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Demarcated erythematous plaque, Localized intense pruritus, Temporal cosmetic correlation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-017
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of intensely itchy, erythematous facial plaque requiring evaluation to rule out facial cellulitis or herpes zoster.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an evolving red, raised patch on the face. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to exclude rapidly spreading infectious processes like facial erysipelas or ophthalmic zoster, preventing potential severe complications.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My cheek turned bright red and is burning and itching uncontrollably after I tried a new face serum.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports the development of a well-demarcated erythematous plaque conforming to the exact application site of a new cosmetic product applied 12 hours ago. Describes localized intense pruritus over the exposed area. Notes fine superficial epidermal scaling and mild edema of the involved dermal tissue. Absence of systemic manifestations such as fever or chills. Complete sparing of non-exposed cutaneous areas.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High cosmetic distress; potential for aggressive inappropriate home remedy applications.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/74 | HR: 76 | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, focused entirely on the facial lesion.
Physical Exam: Facial examination reveals a well-demarcated erythematous plaque with a sharp structural cut-off corresponding directly to the border of cosmetic application. Diagnostic triad confirmed: demarcated erythematous plaque, localized intense pruritus, and a clear temporal correlation to the cosmetic product. No vesicles, crusting, or purulent exudate. Normal periorbital tissue. Absence of regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Newly purchased cosmetic serum bottle present on the vanity.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No history of immunocompromise.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild localized contact dermatitis presenting as an erythematous patch from a new cosmetic product - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Facial Erysipelas/Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, normal localized skin temperature, and exact geometric demarcation matching a topical application.

Herpes Zoster (Shingles) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of unilateral dermatomal distribution, lack of severe neuropathic pain, and no grouped vesicles.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Malar Rash) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute onset tied to a specific cosmetic exposure, unilateral presentation, and absence of systemic joint pain or fatigue.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, expanding facial erythema and edema. Given the anatomical location, it was critical to urgently differentiate an allergic/irritant chemical response from facial cellulitis (erysipelas) which requires immediate antibiotic therapy to prevent cavernous sinus involvement. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period carried unacceptable risk of missed infectious progression. The clinical examination confidently isolated the etiology to a cosmetic contactant, enabling safe, conservative management in the home environment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed to immediately cleanse the face with plain lukewarm water and a gentle, fragrance-free cleanser to remove any residual cosmetic product. Prescription for a low-potency topical corticosteroid (1% hydrocortisone) applied thinly twice daily to the plaque.
Positive Behavioral Change: Perform a "patch test" on the inner forearm for 24 hours with any new cosmetic product to safely verify individual tolerance before applying it to highly visible and sensitive facial skin.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for consideration of formal patch testing if symptoms persist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the swelling spreads to involve the eyelids, if vision is affected, or if you develop difficulty breathing or swallowing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; precise allergen identification requires formal dermatological patch testing.
Clinical Course: The redness and itching should improve significantly within 48-72 hours of stopping the offending agent and applying the topical steroid.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the red area develops painful blisters, if it begins to spread rapidly beyond the original boundary, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A chemical in the new serum triggered an inflammatory response in your skin. The hydrocortisone cream suppresses this immune reaction, stopping the itch and reducing the redness.
Actionable Steps: Stop using the new serum immediately. Apply the steroid cream sparingly twice daily. Avoid hot water, harsh soaps, and makeup until fully healed.

